1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,180
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:05,880
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,600
My name is Catherine and today we're talking about something that has always been very confusing for me.

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:12,920
For me as well.

5
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,560
I don't understand very well what this is about.

6
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:20,860
We're going to be talking about time zones and a little bit about daylight savings time.

7
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:25,480
Before we get started though, let's take a look at this phrase in today's vocabulary preview.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Vocabulary preview.

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,860
All right, so the phrase is daylight savings time.

10
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,340
Okay, so we're trying to save daylight pretty much.

11
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:35,960
That's right.

12
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:40,060
So this is the time of year when we change our clocks.

13
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:41,180
What does that mean?

14
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:46,440
That means in the fall there's a special day and everyone has it on their calendar and we change the clocks.

15
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:48,400
We put them back one hour.

16
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,400
So if it was eight o'clock now it's seven o'clock.

17
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:53,760
Okay, so everything is earlier, starts earlier.

18
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,130
And that's basically to take advantage of the daylight, right?

19
00:00:57,290 --> 00:00:58,970
That's why it's called daylight savings time.

20
00:00:59,110 --> 00:00:59,310
Exactly.

21
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,690
And so that's for the winter and then when the winter's past we change the clocks again.

22
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:07,010
We have the spring daylight savings where we change our clocks ahead.

23
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:07,790
Okay.

24
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:10,010
So it sounds kind of complicated.

25
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:12,650
Many countries don't have daylight savings time.

26
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:14,370
They don't change their clocks at all.

27
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:19,670
But we're going to be talking about this in a little bit more so why don't we listen to our dialogue for the first time.

28
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,120
Did you set your clock forward for daylight savings time?

29
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:28,800
What?

30
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:30,400
Why do we have to do that?

31
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,500
Well, at the start of the spring we usually have more daylight in the mornings and less in the afternoon.

32
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:39,280
This is basically due to our position on the planet and the rotation of the Earth.

33
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:44,960
In any case, to take better advantage of the daylight available we compensate by moving our clocks forward one hour.

34
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:45,760
I see.

35
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:46,640
That's convenient.

36
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,660
I never understood things like this such as GMT.

37
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:53,700
I never know what time zone we are in or when to change my clock.

38
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,260
That just stands for Greenwich Mean Time.

39
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,060
Here in California we're in Pacific Standard Time.

40
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:02,000
That is eight time zones west of Greenwich.

41
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:02,660
Remember when we were in Beijing?

42
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,080
Well, then we were in China Standard Time and that's eight time zones east of Greenwich.

43
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:12,880
That's why it was so weird traveling from Beijing to LA because of the huge time difference.

44
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:20,200
Even though we left Beijing at noon and flew for more than eight hours, we still arrived in LA the same day at noon.

45
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:22,340
It's like we went back in time.

46
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,400
Alright we're back.

47
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:31,020
So that was a pretty clear explanation of why we do these things.

48
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,620
But maybe there are a couple of words that you didn't understand.

49
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,440
So let's take a look at those now in Language Takeaway.

50
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:40,480
Language Takeaway.

51
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:50,280
Well, Marco, what you were hinting at before, this idea that we have shorter days in the winter and longer days in the summer has to do with the rotation of the Earth.

52
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,120
So the rotation of the Earth is our first phrase.

53
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,880
So the Earth rotates.

54
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:56,400
What does that mean if it rotates?

55
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:58,020
Means to spin around.

56
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:00,860
But not just to spin around anywhere.

57
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:06,380
It means that if you draw a line through the middle of something, it never leaves that line.

58
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:08,920
It just kind of turns.

59
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:10,120
So it's spinning.

60
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:11,200
Like the wheels on a car.

61
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:12,760
They rotate.

62
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:17,380
That means they go around but they are fixed to the car.

63
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:23,200
So this is one of the reasons why we need to compensate our clocks because of the rotation of the Earth.

64
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,660
And that's our next word, to compensate.

65
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,860
This is a verb, to compensate.

66
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:32,060
And it's often used with money but it can be used with time as well.

67
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:33,040
Time or sunlight.

68
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:38,760
So with money you'd say, I paid him a hundred dollars but I owed him a hundred fifty.

69
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:41,600
So I'm going to compensate him for the extra fifty.

70
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:42,610
Means I'm going to fix it.

71
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,790
So to compensate would be the verb and compensation would be the noun.

72
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:50,010
Now in this case we're talking about time.

73
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:52,150
So how can we compensate time?

74
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:58,170
Well that means that if we have lost an hour because we changed the clocks, we have to compensate.

75
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,070
We have to try and gain that hour back.

76
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:09,040
And so that's why in the spring, after the days start to get longer and longer, we have to compensate for that by changing the clocks again.

77
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:10,340
So we're compensating time.

78
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:16,440
Now related to daylight savings and this whole time thing is time zones.

79
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,120
We have time zones all over the Earth.

80
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,920
And you've probably noticed this if you've traveled.

81
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:26,500
You have to change your clocks or many times you see in hotels what time it is in London, in Hong Kong, in Berlin, etc.

82
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:38,880
And this makes a lot of sense because like we said, the Earth is rotating and so if it's daylight in Moscow and it's nighttime in New York, obviously it's not the same time.

83
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,100
So to compensate for this difference, we have different time zones.

84
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:48,820
And each time zone is pretty much an hour or so, you know, half an hour different from the next one.

85
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:49,260
Right.

86
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:56,140
So most countries actually have different time zones throughout their country unless they're small that they don't really need to compensate any change.

87
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:01,600
Or for example, China, just to unify everything, it's a big country but they have one time zone.

88
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:03,360
It's China standard time.

89
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:04,360
Exactly.

90
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:05,900
But in the United States, we have four time zones.

91
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:07,280
Right.

92
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:08,280
Alright.

93
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:09,280
So that's time zone.

94
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:12,280
And the other thing related to time zone is time difference.

95
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:14,540
Okay, well think about math here.

96
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,000
Difference is a word that we use in math.

97
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:25,520
And a time difference is the amount of time, that means how many hours there are between one time zone and another time zone.

98
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:28,740
So for example, Beijing and New York City.

99
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:31,000
These are very, very far away from each other.

100
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:34,040
If it's day in New York, it's night in Beijing.

101
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:41,260
So that means if it's seven in the morning in Beijing, it's probably around seven at night in New York.

102
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,300
So the time difference would be more or less 12 hours.

103
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:44,660
Exactly.

104
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:47,840
But when it's daylight savings time, it's 13.

105
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:48,520
So be careful.

106
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:49,720
Right.

107
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:50,200
Okay.

108
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:51,620
I think that's pretty clear.

